{"id": "78809527-f1ae-430f-9cb6-2a7235ecfd24", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T23:10:13.446247", "source": "user", "parent_id": "a60a643a-74aa-46af-9c90-83b196f4890c", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task: we need a fully specified experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility) to test the hypothesis that LLMs exhibit a directional bias on real-world factual parent–child knowledge — strong when the child is given and the parent must be retrieved, weaker when the parent is given and the child must be retrieved. No execution is required and no fabricated results should be reported. Please do the following in this turn only — do NOT draft the full design yet: (1) INSPECT the workspace at /workspace using simple, read-only shell commands (ls -la, getfacl only if helpful, cat on any text files such as track.md and any docs/code present). Report what is there: do not assume; rely only on what `ls`/`cat` actually show. Report the directory structure, the content of track.md verbatim, and the presence/absence of any prior experiment scaffolding, datasets, or prompts. Then briefly check available tooling (e.g., `python3 --version`, `pip3 list` heads) only to inform feasibility — do not install anything, do not download anything, network is disabled. (2) PROPOSE AN APPROACH for the experiment design. Cover at minimum: the selected factual domain and how the parent–child item set will be sourced/curated (Wikipedia-anchored celebrity list is a reasonable default unless you see something better in the repo); the LLM prompting protocol for the two directional conditions (A: child→parent; B: parent→child), including exact prompt templates, ordering control (counterbalanced and randomized), answer extraction rules (exact-match, alias handling, multiple-children cases), and how refusals/non-knowledge will be coded; the controls (paraphrase robustness, distractor set, multiple-children baseline, prompt-order randomization, same-model/self-consistency, optional famous-vs-non-famous fame stratification); the primary metric and supporting metrics (directional accuracy gap, paired test, effect size, bootstrap CIs); a pre-registered analysis plan with hypotheses, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and how you would rule out alternative explanations (name-frequency confounds, surfaceform asymmetry, training-data heaviness toward famous children). (3) LIST ASSUMPTIONS and UNCERTAINTIES that materially shape the design — e.g., assumed LLM access/API availability despite disabled network, assumed ground-truth source (Wikipedia/Wikidata), assumed answer format, handling of step- and adoptive-parent cases, multiple-children ambiguity, how to fix N and reporting power expectations, and what you would trim under a tight budget. Do not propose runtimes, compute, or dollar costs as facts — frame them as assumptions to be confirmed. Do not write the final design document in this turn; I will approve the approach in a follow-up turn. Keep the response strictly to: workspace inspection findings → approach proposal → assumptions/uncertainties.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"For real-world factual data on celebrity parent-child relationships, LLMs will exhibit a directional bias by accurately retrieving a parent when given a child’s name, but will underperform when trying to retrieve the child given the parent.\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\":