{"id": "80ad36d2-f79a-49f2-ba72-5a06babf9e4a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T23:11:29.613238", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "aa424e9a-7f35-4cd4-b53d-0052e5b4e632", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1. Workspace inspection findings (read-only)\n\n**`/workspace` contents** (verified with `ls -la /workspace`):\n```\ndrwx------  2 ... 4096 Oct 23 06:09 .\ndr-xr-x  12 ...  240 Oct 23 06:10 ..\n-rw-------  1 ...  537 Oct 23 06:09 task.json\n```\n\n**Only one file present: `/workspace/task.json`.** Its full content:\n```json\n{\n  \"question\": \"For real-world factual data on celebrity parent–child relationships, LLMs will exhibit a directional bias by accurately retrieving a parent when given a child's name, but will underperform when trying to retrieve the child given the parent.\",\n  \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\",\n  \"documents\": {\n    \"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nObservations grounded in what `ls`/`cat` actually showed:\n- **No `track.md` file exists** under `/workspace`. The string `\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` is the *value* of `documents.track.md` inside `task.json`, not a freestanding markdown file. There is therefore no prior experiment scaffolding, no datasets, no prompt templates, no code, no analysis scripts, no CSV, no notebook in the repo.\n- `/home/actor/` exists and is empty. `/root` and `/tmp` have no work-in-progress.\n- Tooling available: Python 3.12.3 with stdlib (`json, csv, statistics, urllib.request, hashlib, collections, re, random`) all importable. `pip` 24.0 present. Pre-installed packages that matter: `requests` 2.31.0 and `google-api-python-client` 1.7.12. **Not** installed: `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `matplotlib`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `transformers`, `torch`, `httpx`.\n- Network is disabled per the task spec. `which` is not present on PATH, and `curl`/`wget` were not located in the probe.\n- 24 CPUs available; no GPU.\n\nImplication for the design: because the environment does not host an LLM, the experiment design must be **specification-complete at the protocol and analysis level** but cannot be executed here. All references below are to design artifacts the design *requires*, not artifacts that currently exist.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Proposed approach\n\n### 2.1 Factual domain and item set\n\n- **Domain**: real-world celebrity **biological** parent→child relationships. Wikipedia is the appropriate public anchor for surface forms and human-readable verification; **Wikidata (Q5 / P22 / P25 / P40)** is the appropriate structured ground truth (relationship type + offsets + qualifier dates).\n- **Unit of analysis**: a *paired item* — a (parent, child) dyad that can be queried in both directions. Every item yields one observation under condition A and one under condition B, which gives paired-power and naturally controls pair-level confounds (era, nationality, field).\n- **Item set construction (offline, pre-registered)**:\n  1. Seed with parents who have an English Wikipedia article and at least one Wikidata `child (P40)` statement.\n  2. Require the child to also have an English Wikipedia article (so both surface forms are well-attested).\n  3. **Fame stratification**: bin each parent on a tertiary ordinal tier (high / mid / low) using a pre-registered proxy — Wikidata sitelink count of the *parent*, with cutpoints fixed before any model call. Repeat the same binning for the child. Record both, so directional effects can be decomposed by \"parent famous\" vs \"child famous\".\n  4. **Inclusion**: biological relations whose qualifier dates match the parent's article (sourced from Wikidata); step- and adoptive-relations *excluded* from the primary set, but kept in a **sensitivity arm** (see 2.6).\n  5. **Exclusion**: (a) currently-living children under 18, (b) disputed or contested parentage flags on Wikidata, (c) dyads where parent and child names share an exact token (e.g., \"Knox Jolie-Pitt\" / \"Jon Voight\" — name-overlap cases are noted but not excluded in primary analysis; an alias-overlap sub-study is added to control for surface-form confound).\n  6. **Singleton-child subset**: from the full paired set, derive a **multiple-children-controlled subset** containing only parents with exactly one Wikidata-attested child. This is the cleanest comparison for the primary metric.\n  7. **Distractor match**: for each pair, pre-collect K≈3 sibling distractors — other parents in the same fame tier whose names are length- and alphabet-similar — to enable a forced-choice control (see 2.3).\n  8. **Reporting target N**: aim for ≥150 paired items across fame tiers, with ≥50 in the singleton-child subset. Power / sample-size justification deferred until item counts are concretized (treated as an assumption, §3).\n\n### 2.2 LLM prompting protocol — two directional conditions\n\n- **Condition A (child → parent)**: prompt the model to retrieve the *biological* parent(s) given the child.\n- **Condition B (parent → child)**: prompt the model to retrieve the *biological* child(ren) given the parent.\n- **Counterbalancing**: every dyad receives both conditions. Condition order per item is randomized; prompt-template order is randomized within each condition. Two independently-randomized orderings are stored and re-used for any temperature>0 sampling.\n- **Paraphrase robustness**: each condition has a pre-registered pair of paraphrases (open-ended \"Who is the mother/father of X?\" and templated \"X's parents are:\"). Paraphrase is a within-item control, not a hypothesis test.\n- **Knowledge-probe phrasing (within each direction)**:\n  - Free-form: \"Name one biological child of PARENT.\" / \"Name one biological parent of CHILD.\"\n  - Multiple-choice (forced-choice control): \"Which of the following is a biological parent of CHILD? (a)… (b)… (c)… (d)…\" — distractors from §2.1.7.\n  - \"Don't know\" surfaced as a labeled option for refusal-rate coding.\n- **Prompt template family** (exact templates to be ratified in the design):\n  - A_open: \"Among the following names, identify the biological parent of {CHILD}:\\n{a. T1, b. T2, c. T3, d. T4}\\nRespond with a single letter or 'I don't know'.\"\n  - A_free: \"Who is a biological parent of {CHILD}? Respond with exactly one full name, or 'I don't know'.\"\n  - B_open / B_free: symmetric.\n- **Decoding**: temperature 0 (greedy) for the primary run; **a separate K-sample arm** (e.g., K=5 at T=1.0) for self-consistency, kept *out* of the primary hypothesis test to avoid doubling.\n\n### 2.3 Answer extraction and coding\n\n- **Normalization**: lowercase; collapse diacritics; strip honorifics; collapse whitespace.\n- **Alias table**: a human-curated alias map (maiden names, stage names, transliterations) anchored to each surface form; pre-registered and locked before grading.\n- **Scoring decisions** (each item × condition yields):\n  - `correct_top1`: matches a ground-truth target within the alias table.\n  - `correct_topk`: k∈{3, all} — correct if any retrieved entity is in the canonical set.\n  - `refusal`: model emits \"I don't know\" / equivalent hedge-lexicon match.\n  - `non_knowledge`: refusal + no on-list entities returned.\n  - `multiple_correct`: in B with |children|>1, model names *any one* canonical child.\n- **Multiple-children handling** for condition B: ground truth is the *full* Wikidata `child (P40)` list for the parent (deduplicated). `correct_top1` requires a name from that list; `correct_any` (\"name any one\") is reported alongside; **multiple-correct metric is the headline only within the singleton-child subset** to avoid automatic easy-pass for big families.\n\n### 2.4 Controls\n\n| Control | What it rules out |\n|---|---|\n| **Paraphrase robustness** (2 prompt phrasings) | Phrasing-specific artifacts |\n| **Distractor / forced-choice** (multiple-choice arm) | Word-recall vs entity-recognition confound |\n| **Singleton-child subset** | Multiple-children ambiguity inflating B's denominator |\n| **Prompt-order randomization** (two orderings) | Order effects |\n| **Same-model self-consistency** (K-sample arm) | Decoding stochasticity; majority-vote sensitivity |\n| **Fame stratification** (parent/child tiers) | Training-data heaviness as a sole explanation |\n| **Surface-form length / uniqueness matched subsample** | Name-frequency / surfaceform asymmetry |\n| **Alias-overlap sub-study** (items where parent & child share tokens) | Token-overlap shortcut explanations |\n\n### 2.5 Primary metric, supporting metrics, paired test\n\n- **Primary endpoint**: directional accuracy gap = `Acc_A(singleton_subset) − Acc_B(singleton_subset)`, computed at the singleton-child subset for the headline number; identical metric on the full paired set is reported as Supporting.\n- **Statistical test**: **McNemar's test** on the per-item (A-correct?, B-correct?) 2×2 within the singleton subset (planned primary); **Wilcoxon signed-rank** on per-item binary correctness across the full set as a sensitivity test. Both one-sided in the pre-registered direction (Acc_A > Acc_B).\n- **Effect size**: Cohen's *h* for paired proportions; odds ratio from McNemar; Cohen's *d* on the per-item binary difference for Wilcoxon.\n- **Uncertainty**: **stratified bootstrap 95% CI** (B=10,000) on the gap, resampling at the dyad level, stratified by parent-fame tier.\n- **Supporting metrics**: top-k accuracy (k=1, 3, ∞), per-direction refusal rate, self-consistency (fraction of K samples identical to the top-1), mean reciprocal rank (free-form B only), per-tier gap, forced-choice gap.\n\n### 2.6 Pre-registered analysis plan\n\n- **Hypotheses** (one-sided, pre-registered):\n  - **H1**: `P(correct | A, item) > P(correct | B, item)` averaged over items in the singleton subset.\n  - **H0 (directional null)**: `P(A) ≤ P(B)`.\n- **Inclusion criteria**: items passing §2.1.5 inclusion; ground truth confirmed in Wikidata before any model call.\n- **Exclusion criteria**: refusals are **not** dropped — they are coded and reported (refusal-as-incorrect for the primary metric; refusal-rate is reported as a supporting metric).\n- **Alternative-explanation checks, pre-registered**:\n  1. **Name-frequency / surfaceform asymmetry**: recompute the gap on items matched on parent name length (and on Wikidata sitelink-count percentile).\n  2. **Training-data heaviness toward famous children**: recompute within each parent-fame tier and child-fame tier separately; if the gap survives the low-fame tier, training-data-heaviness alone cannot account for it.\n  3. **Multiple-children confound**: primary metric is the singleton-subset gap; report the full-set gap transparently alongside.\n  4. **Alias / token overlap**: rerun excluding name-overlap items; report direction of change.\n  5. **Step- / adoptive-parent sensitivity arm**: items with non-biological qualifiers are scored separately and reported.\n- **Multiplicity**: pre-specify the singleton-subset gap as the single primary endpoint; all others are exploratory/supporting with Holm correction across the secondary metrics.\n- **Failures that falsify H1 cleanly**: either a non-positive point estimate of the gap, or a 95% bootstrap CI excluding any positive effect size.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Assumptions (separate from task requirements)\n\n- **A1. Model access is not available** in this bubblewrap environment. The design assumes an external LLM API or local model is selectable by the researcher; the design only specifies protocol and analysis. *Not a fact: no costs, runtime, or vendor are committed.*\n- **A2. Ground truth source**: Wikidata Q5/P22/P25/P40 with Wikipedia prose cross-checks, accessed before any model call (offline item-set construction).\n- **A3. Answer format** is short free-text (single name); we additionally permit a forced-choice arm per §2.2.\n- **A4. Step-/adoptive-parent cases** are excluded from the primary set and isolated in a sensitivity arm; biological-only is the default.\n- **A5. Multiple-children ambiguity** is handled by (a) restricting the primary endpoint to the singleton-child subset and (b) reporting `correct_any` plus top-k on the full set.\n- **A6. N and power**: assume ≥150 paired items full-set and ≥50 singleton-subset items; power is revisited after item-set construction. Bootstrap CIs are reported regardless.\n- **A7. Self-consistency arm** uses K=5 at T=1.0, and is *not* part of the primary hypothesis test.\n- **A8. Fame-tier proxy**: Wikidata sitelink count of the parent's English Wikipedia article, with tertiles fixed before any model call.\n- **A9.** Headline metric uses **temperature 0** decoding.\n- **A10. Budget-trim path** (if everything is squeezed): drop paraphrases, drop the multiple-choice arm, drop the K-sample arm, drop the adoptive sensitivity arm; keep primary endpoint, singleton subset, fame stratification, bootstrap CI.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Uncertainties (material to the design)\n\n- **U1. Whether the model emits aliases (e.g., maiden names) vs canonical surface forms** — affects alias table size and scoring sensitivity. *Resolution: lock alias table from Wikidata surface forms + Wikipedia lead paragraph before grading.*\n- **U2. Whether the model's notion of \"celebrity\" matches the items set's fame tiers** — affects whether low-fame arm is interpretable. *Resolution: require each tier to have ≥20 items in the singleton subset.*\n- **U3. Whether refusal-rate asymmetry itself drives the gap** — uncodable refusals could mimic the effect. *Resolution: report refusal-rate per direction; rerun with refusals scored as \"correct\" in a sensitivity analysis.*\n- **U4. Multiple-children ambiguity in B**: for large families, top-k can be near-trivial. *Resolution: singleton subset is primary; full set reported with `correct_any` and MRR.*\n- **U5. Surface-form confound** that is *unrelated to frequency*: e.g., distinguishing \"Cary Grant\" from \"Judy Garland\" via atypical tokens. *Resolution: token-overlap sub-study.*\n- **U6. Decoding temperature sensitivity** without GPU/deterministic mode — *Resolution: temperature 0 primary; K-sample at T=1.0 reported only as exploratory.*\n- **U7. Authoritative number of famous parents with exactly one well-attested biological child plus Wikipedia-anchored child** — *Resolution: pilot the construction step before registering N.*\n- **U8. Whether the gap, if observed, is *directional* or *degenerative* (a floor effect in B) — *Resolution: floor-effect diagnostic: report B's accuracy conditioned on items where siblings have lower headline-fame than the canonical child.*\n- **U9. Whether step-/adoptive qualifiers on Wikidata are consistently tagged — *Resolution: report label-cleanliness rate; restrict sensitive items whose qualifier is unambiguous.*\n\n---\n\nI have **not** drafted the full design document, runtimes, or any result. Approve this approach (or flag changes), and I will produce the full design in the next turn at `stage=result`.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection:\n- `ls -la /workspace` showed a single file `/workspace/task.json` (537 bytes) and no other files or subdirectories.\n- `find` for *.md/*.txt/*.py/*.json/*.yaml/*.csv returned only `/workspace/task.json`.\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` showed no `track.md` file on disk; the string \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" exists only as the value of `documents.track.md` inside `task.json`.\n- `/home/actor/` exists and is empty; `/root` and `/tmp` contain no work-in-progress.\nTooling probe:\n- Python 3.12.3, stdlib fully importable; `pip` 24.0 available.\n- Pre-installed relevant packages: `requests` 2.31.0, `google-api-python-client` 1.7.12.\n- Not installed: `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `matplotlib`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `transformers`, `torch`, `httpx`.\n- 24 CPUs; no GPU; `which` not on PATH; `curl`/`wget` not located; network disabled per task spec.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-ef91a1b9-2143-458f-b0b8-ea3224643ed1", "tool_call": {"id": "call-ef91a1b9-2143-458f-b0b8-ea3224643ed1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Plan: directional parent-child retrieval experiment\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## 1. Workspace inspection findings (read-only)\\n\\n**`/workspace` contents** (verified with `ls -la /workspace`):\\n```\\ndrwx------  2 ... 4096 Oct 23 06:09 .\\ndr-xr-x  12 ...  240 Oct 23 06:10 ..\\n-rw-------  1 ...  537 Oct 23 06:09 task.json\\n```\\n\\n**Only one file present: `/workspace/task.json`.** Its full content:\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"For real-world factual data on celebrity parent\\u2013child relationships, LLMs will exhibit a directional bias by accurately retrieving a parent when given a child's name, but will underperform when trying to retrieve the child given the parent.\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": {\\n    \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nObservations grounded in what `ls`/`cat` actually showed:\\n- **No `track.md` file exists** under `/workspace`. The string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` is the *value* of `documents.track.md` inside `task.json`, not a freestanding markdown file. There is therefore no prior experiment scaffolding, no datasets, no prompt templates, no code, no analysis scripts, no CSV, no notebook in the repo.\\n- `/home/actor/` exists and is empty. `/root` and `/tmp` have no work-in-progress.\\n- Tooling available: Python 3.12.3 with stdlib (`json, csv, statistics, urll