{"id": "cd726ac3-77a9-49c0-a5e9-2daa39b84f80", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T23:12:00.452118", "source": "user", "parent_id": "f90ce669-56fa-4ad1-af38-cf871417e3c1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Move to the final design stage and produce a single, self-contained experiment design document covering, at minimum: (a) item-set construction protocol end-to-end, including Wikidata SPARQL queries or equivalent offline fetch rules, alias-table curation, fame-tier proxy with cutpoints, inclusion/exclusion rules, and an explicit feasibility estimate for N (>=150 paired + >=50 singleton) before any model call, with a documented fallback if the singleton-subset yield falls short; (b) verbatim prompt templates for A_open, A_free, B_open, B_free and the forced-choice variants, plus the exact order-randomization and counterbalancing scheme; (c) normalization/alias matching code-in-words with edge cases (maiden names, transliterations, 'Jr.'/'III', hyphenated surnames), and the exact refusal-coding lexicon; (d) the pre-registered analysis plan from the approach rendered as a single locked analysis specification, with H1/H0 wording, primary endpoint (singleton-subset gap), McNemar primary test with one-sided alpha, Wilcoxon sensitivity, Cohen's h, odds ratio, stratified bootstrap 95% CI, and Holm-corrected secondary metrics; (e) the alternative-explanation checklist from the approach, written as five concrete rerun specifications rather than a list; (f) a feasibility section stating dataset construction effort, expected API calls per cell, wall-clock at assumed T=0, K-sample cost, total items to be queried, and the budget-trim path from A10 kept intact as the contingency plan; (g) an explicit statement that no experiment has been run, with placeholders for any future-run diagnostics. Do not fabricate results, runtimes, or vendor pricing; describe ranges or 'to be measured' where appropriate. Constraints reminder: the bubblewrap environment cannot execute an LLM, so all references to run execution must remain conditional on the researcher's external setup. Deliver the design as the final candidate.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 24}
{"id": "2cdf9900-701d-49ec-937b-cc450a75f890", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T23:13:31.353192", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "cd726ac3-77a9-49c0-a5e9-2daa39b84f80", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Directional Bias in LLM Parent–Child Retrieval\n\n**Status:** Design only. **No experiment has been executed.** No results, runtimes, vendor pricing, or compute claims are committed; ranges are stated as \"to be measured under the researcher's external LLM access.\" Item-set construction depends on offline Wikidata/Wikipedia fetches performed by the researcher; this bubblewrap environment only contains `task.json` (537 bytes) and has no LLM access, no network, no GPU.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Locked design identifiers\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Public hypothesis | LLMs accurately retrieve a parent from a child's name more often than vice versa. |\n| Domain | Celebrity **biological** parent–child relations. |\n| Primary endpoint | `Acc_A(singleton_subset) − Acc_B(singleton_subset)`. |\n| Primary test | McNemar, one-sided, α = 0.025 (Bonferroni split between primary + Wilcoxon sensitivity). |\n| Headline N | ≥150 paired items full set, ≥50 in singleton-child subset. |\n| Headline decoding | temperature 0. |\n| Truth source | Wikidata (P40 child, P22 father, P25 mother) cross-checked against Wikipedia prose. |\n\nAll numbered §1–§11 sections below are **pre-registered**; any later change requires an amendment log entry.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Assumptions carried over (locked)\n\n- **A1.** LLM access is external to bubblewrap; all execution is conditional on the researcher's setup.\n- **A2.** Ground truth = Wikidata `child (P40)` / `father (P22)` / `mother (P25)`, English Wikipedia prose for verification, accessed **before any model call** (item-set construction step).\n- **A3.** Free-text answer format is a single full name; forced-choice arm uses single-letter response.\n- **A4.** Step-/adoptive relations are **excluded from primary** and isolated in a sensitivity arm.\n- **A5.** Multiple-children ambiguity is handled by (i) singleton-subset primary endpoint, (ii) `correct_any` and top-k for the full set.\n- **A6.** N target: ≥150 paired items full / ≥50 singleton; power revisited after item-set is built.\n- **A7.** K-sample arm uses K=5 at T=1.0; **not** in primary hypothesis test.\n- **A8.** Fame-tier proxy = Wikidata sitelink count of the **parent's** English Wikipedia article; tertiles locked before any model call.\n- **A9.** Headline decoding = temperature 0.\n- **A10. Budget-trim path:** drop paraphrases → drop forced-choice → drop K-sample → drop adoptive sensitivity; **always keep** primary endpoint, singleton subset, fame stratification, and bootstrap CI.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Item-set construction protocol (offline, pre-registered)\n\n### 2.1 Seed and relation extraction (Wikidata primary)\n\nPerformed once, offline, before any model call. Structured data is canonical ground truth; Wikipedia prose is the verification side-channel. Both are pulled **before** the alias table is built.\n\n**SPARQL template — Pass 1: parents with at least one child, English-Wikipedia-attested.**\n\n```sparql\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?parentSitelinks\n       ?child  ?childLabel  ?childSitelinks\n       ?father ?fatherLabel ?mother ?motherLabel\nWHERE {\n  ?parent wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .                                      # human\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .                                     # has child\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?father .                                    # parent's father\n  ?parent wdt:P25 ?mother .\n  ?parentArticle schema:about ?parent ;                       # English Wikipedia article exists\n                schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/> .\n  ?childArticle  schema:about ?child  ;\n                 schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/> .\n  ?parentArticle wikibase:rank wikibase:PreferredRank .\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \"en\" . }\n}\n```\n\n`?parentSitelinks` / `?childSitelinks` come from `wdt:P3744` indirectly via `?parent wdt:P3744 ?parentSitelinks` (English Wikipedia sitelink count) — rejoin in a second sub-query if needed (Wikidata SPARQL does not consistently serve P3744 over the public endpoint).\n\n**SPARQL template — Pass 2: filter to celebrity-adjacent occupations.**\n\n```sparql\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?child ?childLabel WHERE {\n  VALUES ?occ { wd:Q33999 wd:Q177220 wd:Q483501 wd:Q10800557 wd:Q158852 }   # actor, singer, musician, film director, entertainer\n  ?parent wdt:P106 ?occ .\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?f; wdt:P25 ?m .\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \"en\" . }\n}\n```\n\n`Q33999` actor, `Q177220` singer, `Q483501` artist, `Q10800557` film director, `Q158852` entertainer — values may be amended at item-set pilot time, but the value list is locked before grader code is committed.\n\n**Fame-tier proxy cutpoints.** Use the empirical tertiles of `parentSitelinks` from Pass 1 over the universe of parents with at least one Wikipedia article and a child relation. **Lock the cutpoints numerically** (e.g., τ_low, τ_high) in the design log *prior* to any model call. Recomputation is forbidden post-hoc.\n\n### 2.2 Pass 3: ground-truth child enumeration\n\n```sparql\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?child ?childLabel WHERE {\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?f; wdt:P25 ?m .\n  ?child schema:description ?desc . FILTER(LANG(?desc) = \"en\")\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \"en\" . }\n}\n```\n\nApply `LIMIT 50000` and materialize locally to CSV. Sort parents by `parentSitelinks`.\n\n### 2.3 Alias-table curation (human-in-the-loop, locked before grading)\n\nFor every surface form that appears in (parent, child) positions, generate the canonical alias set:\n\n1. Start with Wikidata `skos:altLabel`, `rdfs:label`, and all \"also known as\" qualifiers.\n2. Additionally pull the lead paragraph of each subject's English Wikipedia article; manually extract (a) birth name, (b) stage name, (c) married/maiden surnames, (d) transliterations, (e) explicit \"Jr.\", \"Sr.\", \"II\", \"III\" tokens.\n3. Manual limit: at most **2 human-curators**; conflicts resolved before the design log is sealed.\n4. Alias map is **immutable** once stored — any grader that mutates it is a bug by definition.\n\n### 2.4 Item-set inclusion / exclusion\n\n**Inclusion (an item qualifies only if all hold):**\n- Parent and child each have an English Wikipedia article.\n- Both have independent Wikidata items of class `human` (Q5).\n- A biographical parent–child statement is present in Wikidata (`parent has child` via P40 OR `child has parent` via P22/P25).\n- The child is the **first biological child** listed for that parent in the materialized P40 list, *or* the item is marked into the singleton-subset path (see 2.5).\n\n**Exclusion (any one is sufficient):**\n- Currently-living child under 18.\n- Disputed or contested parentage flag on the Wikidata statement.\n- Adoptive- or step-only relations on P22/P25/P40 qualifiers.\n- The parent or child shares an exact non-honorific token with their counterpart (handled in a sub-study, §6).\n- The child is more famous than the parent in terms of tertile (these items are flagged but **not automatically excluded**; they are required for the §5.2 fame-tier sensitivity).\n\n### 2.5 Singleton-child subset\n\nA parent is in the singleton set iff the materialized P40 list (Pass 3) is non-empty and has exactly one item. This subset is the **primary-endpoint population**.\n\n### 2.6 Forced-choice distractors\n\nFor each item, choose K=3 sibling distractors: closest other parents (or siblings of the parent in the matched-child direction) by (i) same parent-fame tertile, (ii) name-length within ±3 tokens, (iii) alphabet prefix distance `< 3 letters`. Reject distractors whose length-normalized Levenshtein distance to the gold is `< 0.3` (to avoid giving answer away by lexical similarity). Locked before grading.\n\n### 2.7 Feasibility gate (must be satisfied before any model call)\n\nBefore the first LLM call, item-set construction must report:\n\n- `N_full = |paired items| ≥ 150`. **Fallback if not met:** broaden the occupation VALUES list in Pass 2, then relax the Wikipedia article requirement on the **child** only. Re-evaluate.\n- `N_single = |items with |children|=1| ≥ 50`. **Fallback if not met:** add the dominant-tertile \"celebrity-couple-with-one-child\" anchor list (pre-prepared), with the relaxation annotated in the design log.\n- All aliases ready and immutable. Pair and distractor lists sealed.\n- Fame-tier cutpoints numerical and locked.\n\nIf both `N_full ≥ 150` and `N_single ≥ 50` cannot be reached under the documented relaxations, the experiment is **not run** and the design is amended.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prompting protocol and counterbalancing\n\n### 3.1 Verbatim prompt templates\n\n```\nA_open:\n  \"Among the following names, identify the biological parent of {CHILD}:\\n\n   (a) {D1}    (b) {D2}    (c) {D3}    (d) {D_GOLD}\\n\n   Respond with a single letter (a, b, c, or d) or 'I don't know'.\\n\n   If you cannot identify a parent, respond 'I don't know'.\"\n\nA_free:\n  \"Who is a biological parent of {CHILD}? Respond with exactly one full name,\n   or 'I don't know'. Do not write any other text.\"\n\nB_open:\n  \"Among the following names, identify one biological child of {PARENT}:\\n\n   (a) {D1}    (b) {D2}    (c) {D3}    (d) {D_GOLD}\\n\n   Respond with a single letter (a, b, c, or d) or 'I don't know'.\\n\n   If you cannot identify a child, respond 'I don't know'.\"\n\nB_free:\n  \"Name one biological child of {PARENT}. Respond with exactly one full name,\n   or 'I don't know'. Do not write any other text.\"\n```\n\nWhere `{D1..D3}` are randomized per-item via §3.2, and `{D_GOLD}` is the canonical answer. In `{D_GOLD}` *not* placed at fixed position d; its slot is randomized among {a, b, c, d} per query to neutralize position bias. Gold is never co-located with its known alias in `{D1..D3}` (verified by alias-overlay check at item-construction time, §2.6).\n\nKnowledge-probe framing language is **identical** across directions modulo the parent/child substitution — condition differences cannot be attributed to word choice.\n\n### 3.2 Counterbalancing and randomization scheme\n\n| Knob | Setting |\n|---|---|\n| Conditions per item | Both A and B (paired design). |\n| Phrasings per condition | `open` and `free`; both rendered. |\n| Direction order per item | Independently random Bernoulli(0.5): A-first or B-first. |\n| Phrasing order per item | Independently random Bernoulli(0.5). |\n| Gold position in forced-choice | Uniform over {a, b, c, d} per query, redrawn per item. |\n| Item presentation order | Two pre-generated orderings (`order_v1`, `order_v2`), for cross-order robustness reporting. |\n| Distractor slot fill | Two pre-generated distractor shuffles (`shuf_v1`, `shuf_v2`) per item. |\n| Decoding | T=0 primary; K=5 @ T=1.0 secondary (held out of primary). |\n\nTwo pre-generated global orderings are stored; **the same orderings are used for any temperature>0 sampling arm** — no on-the-fly reordering.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Normalization, alias matching, refusal coding (code-in-words)\n\n### 4.1 Normalization pipeline (applied identically to model output and to every alias in the table)\n\n1. Unicode → NFKD; **drop combining marks only** (preserves diacritics that are part of the canonical form, e.g., `Ł`).\n2. Lowercase the entire string.\n3. Strip leading/trailing whitespace; collapse internal whitespace to a single ASCII space. (NB: pre-normalization step ran **before** lowercasing only on honorifics; lowercase comparison afterward.)\n4. Strip leading honorifics from a closed set: `{mr, mrs, ms, miss, dr, sir, dame, lord, lady, prof, mx}`.\n5. Strip trailing generation tokens from a closed set: `{jr, sr, ii, iii, iv, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, the 2nd, the 3rd, the 4th}` after lowercasing.\n6. For hyphenated surnames: collapse `\\s*-\\s*` to `-`; **do not** drop the hyphen.\n7. Strip a trailing comma + leading \"phD\", \"md\" etc. from a closed qualifier set.\n8. Drop trailing punctuation `. , ; :`.\n9. Compare to **every** alias in the locked alias table for that subject; treat equality as a match.\n\n### 4.2 Edge cases (explicit rules)\n\n- **Maiden/married names**: handled via alias table; the alias set for a subject contains *every* surname under which that person is plausibly known (e.g., \"Smith\", \"Jones-Smith\", \"Smith-Jones\" — all must be cataloged at item-set time, §2.3).\n- **Transliterations**: include common variants (\"Yelena\" / \"Elena\"), but only those the human curator has explicitly added to the locked alias set. **No dynamic transliteration inference at grading time.**\n- **`Jr.`, `Sr.`, `II`, `III`, `IV`**: stripped from output by step 4.1.5; if a model *adds* a generation token that the canonical form does not carry, it does **not** invalidate the match (e.g., \"Martin Sheen\" accepted as answer to \"Ramon Estevez's father\"; generation tokens don't trigger mismatch).\n- **Hyphenated surnames**: collapsed as in 4.1.6; compare token-wise after collapse.\n- **Stage names**: handled by alias table inclusion, not by string heuristics.\n- **Initials** (\"J. K. Rowling\"): only accepted if the alias table contains the corresponding expanded form.\n\n### 4.3 Refusal-coding lexicon\n\nA response is coded `REFUSAL` iff it contains (case-insensitive substring match) **any** of:\n\n`i don't know`, `i do not know`, `unknown`, `not sure`, `cannot identify`, `can't identify`, `can't tell`, `cannot tell`, `no information`, `no idea`, `unclear`, `unsure`, `not familiar`, `don't have`, `do not have`, `insufficient information`, `n/a`, `na`, `i'm not aware`, `i am not aware`.\n\n**Edge rule:** if the response both contains a refusal-cue **and** a name, **the name takes precedence** — code as a named answer (which may then be correct, incorrect, or non-knowledge). Refusal-rate is reported separately as a supporting metric.\n\n### 4.4 Forced-choice grading\n\nFor `_open` variants, dependencies on the lexicon above are flagged in the design log: \"D\" / \"I don't know\" responses in `_open` are `REFUSAL` only when the letter is also literally `d` paired with a refusal phrase; otherwise they are coded as a letter → graded against the gold slot.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Pre-registered analysis specification (single locked spec)\n\n### 5.1 Notation\n\n- `i ∈ I_single`: items in the **singleton-child subset**; `|I_single| = N_single ≥ 50`.\n- For each `i ∈ I_single`, let `a_i = 1` if A_free graded correct, else `0`; `b_i = 1` if B_free graded correct, else `0`. Forced-choice variants grade analogously: `a^o_i`, `b^o_i`.\n- Refusal ≠ correct: `REFUSAL` graded as `0` for the primary metric. Sensitivity: §5.5.\n\n### 5.2 Pre-registered hypotheses\n\n- **H1** (one-sided): the per-item accuracy gap is positive on the singleton-child subset:\n  `Δ = P(a_i = 1) − P(b_i = 1) > 0`, averaged over `i ∈ I_single`.\n- **H0** (directional null): `Δ ≤ 0`.\n\n### 5.3 Primary endpoint\n\n**`gap = mean_i a_i − mean_i b_i`** over `i ∈ I_single`, computed on the `free`-form variant.\n\n### 5.4 Primary statistical test\n\n- **Test:** McNemar's test on the 2×2 of within-item correctness pairs `(a_i, b_i)` over `I_single`, with continuity correction, **one-sided**, **α = 0.025** (Bonferroni-split with the Wilcoxon sensitivity test, α_total = 0.05).\n- **Effect size:** Cohen's *h* = `2·arcsin(√mean(a)) − 2·arcsin(√mean(b))`; report odds ratio from McNemar's discordant pair counts `(b, n_b)` and `(c, n_c)` as `OR = n_b / n_c` with exact (small-sample) CI.\n- **Uncertainty:** stratified bootstrap 95% CI on `gap`, **B = 10,000** resamples of items, stratified by parent-fame tertile; CI reported to 3 decimals.\n\n### 5.5 Secondary tests (sensitivity, not inferentially protected against primary multiplicity)\n\n- **Wilcoxon signed-rank** on `(a_i − b_i)` over `I_single`, one-sided, α = 0.025.\n- Same gap on the **full paired set** `I_full` (report point estimate + bootstrap CI).\n- Same gap computed on **forced-choice** (`_open`) responses.\n- **Paired gap on `open − free`** to detect any direction-conditioned sensitivity to prompt form.\n\n### 5.6 Holm-corrected secondary metrics (reject at familywise α = 0.05)\n\nOrder these six by descending raw-p; apply Holm sequentially:\n\n1. Refusal-rate asymmetry `R_A − R_B` over `I_single` (McNemar on refusal events).\n2. Top-3 accuracy gap on `I_full` for `B_free` (`correct_top3`).\n3. Mean reciprocal rank gap on `B_free` over `I_full` (Wilcoxon).\n4. Self-consistency rate per item over `I_single` (kl > 0.5 of the K samples equal to the greedy answer; reported as paired proportions test).\n5. Forced-choice gap on `I_single`.\n6. Gap per parent-fame tertile (3 tests; corrected within tertiary family by min-p over the three tertiles).\n\n### 5.7 Decision rules\n\n- **H1 supported** iff (i) the McNemar one-sided p < 0.025, (ii) the bootstrap 95% CI's lower bound > 0, and (iii) the direction of `gap` is positive on the full set as well (positive but possibly non-significant is acceptable to report as a supporting consistency check).\n- **H1 falsified** if any of: gap ≤ 0 on `I_single`, McNemar p ≥ 0.025, lower bootstrap CI bound ≤ 0.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Alternative-explanation rerun specifications (concrete, not a list)\n\nEach rerun takes the **locked** item set and applies a defined filter; reruns are pre-registered as a fixed set, not adaptive.\n\n**R1 — Surface-form asymmetry check.**\nRestrict `I_single` to pairs whose `(parent name token count, child name token count)` lie within a ±1 band; cutoff locked at item-set time. Recompute primary `gap` and McNemar's test. *Pass criterion:* primary `gap` direction is unchanged on the matched-name-length set. *Annotated as a control for surface-form asymmetry without claiming exact control.*\n\n**R2 — Fame-tier decomposition.**\nPartition `I_single` into the locked parent-fame tertiles; compute `gap_τ` per tertile; report min-tertile `gap` and its bootstrap CI. *Pass criterion:* `gap` is non-negative in the bottom tertile (low-fame) — this is what would qualify the result as evidence against the \"training-data heaviness toward famous children\" alternative.\n\n**R3 — Token-overlap sub-study.**\nSub-population `I_overlap` = items where parent and child share ≥ 1 non-honorific token (e.g., \"King\" / \"King Elizabeth II\"). Compute `gap_overlap`. *Pass criterion:* `gap_overlap` direction is unchanged; magnitude reported for transparency.\n\n**R4 — Singleton-subset vs full-set gap.**\nCompute `gap_full` on `I_full` with `correct_any` grading for B (name any one canonical child for `|children| > 1`). *Pass criterion:* `gap_full ≥ gap_single · 0.5` is reported as a magnitude comparison only — no significance claim. (If `gap_full` shrinks to ≤ 0, this becomes a *flagged* finding, not a falsification.)\n\n**R5 — Refusal-as-correct sensitivity.**\nRecompute `gap_R` after grading `REFUSAL` as `1` (alternatively as `0`, the primary). *Pass criterion:* `gap_R` direction is preserved in both coding choices. *Special interpretation:* if direction flips, the effect is confounded with refusal *rate* asymmetry; report and re-evaluate.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility section (no fabricated figures)\n\n### 7.1 Item-set construction effort\n\n- **Wikidata SPARQL passes:** 3, run offline; each on the order of minutes to tens of minutes depending on the public endpoint's load. Local CSV materialization: minutes. Total offline construction: a half-day of human researcher time including alias curation; documented in the design log.\n- **Manual alias curation effort:** proportional to |distinct subjects| ≈ 2 × `N_full`. At target `N_full = 150`, expect ≈ 300 subjects → ≈ 4–6 hours of human curation time.\n- **Fame-tier tertile computation:** one offline pass; cutpoints locked numerically once computed.\n\n### 7.2 LLM query budget (uncertainty is intentional)\n\nThe design does not commit a vendor or rate. Cell counts below are derived purely from the protocol; **per-call response-time and tokens are to be measured under the researcher's external setup**.\n\n| Cell | Items | Phrasings | Direction order | Distractor shufs | Calls per item | Total calls |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Headline T=0, `_free` | `N_full` = ~150 | 1 | 2 | n/a | 2 (one A, one B) | ~300 |\n| Headline T=0, `_open` | `N_full` | 1 | 2 | 2 | 4 (2 directions × 2 shufs) | ~1,200 |\n| K-sample T=1.0, K=5 | `N_full` | 1 (`_free`) | 1 (worst-order only) | n/a | 10 | ~1,500 |\n| Sensitivity (R1–R5) | subsets, no new queries | — | — | — | 0 | 0 |\n\n**Total model calls** (sum, headline only): ~3,000 calls before the K-sample arm; ~**4,500 with the K-sample arm** — *to be measured* under the researcher's setup.\n\n`N_single ≈ 50` contributes same per-item call counts; totals above are dominated by `N_full`.\n\n### 7.3 Wall-clock estimate (T=0, primary)\n\nWall-clock = total calls × per-call latency. Per-call latency is **not measured** in bubblewrap; treat it as α-seconds-per-call and report `(α × 4,500)` seconds as a parametric placeholder once α is measured by the researcher. No commit to seconds, tokens, or dollars.\n\n### 7.4 K-sample cost\n\nK = 5 at T = 1.0. Per-item K-sample cost = `K × per-call latency × per-call tokens`. Held **out** of the primary hypothesis test (§A7). Trimmable under §A10.\n\n### 7.5 Total items to be queried\n\nIncluding both directions and both phrasings: `2 × N_full × (1 free + 2 open shufs) = ~1,500 single-direction queries` before any sensitivity. K-sample: an additional `K × N_full = 750` warm-cache-tolerant queries.\n\n### 7.6 Budget-trim path (A10, kept intact)\n\nOrder of cuts; each cut is **all-or-nothing across the experiment**:\n\n1. Drop paraphrase robustness (only `_free` retained). Saves ~50% of the headine call count.\n2. Drop forced-choice variant entirely (no `A_open`/`B_open`). Saves the ~1,200 forced-choice calls.\n3. Drop K-sample arm. Saves ~1,500 calls.\n4. Drop adoptive / step sensitivity arm (which is already excluded in primary; this refers to dropping the §6 R5 sensitivity if recoded).\n5. **Never cut:** primary endpoint on `I_single`, fame stratification, bootstrap CI.\n\nThe minimum viable experiment requires only headine `_free` T=0 over `N_single ≥ 50` items, both directions, item-randomized; ~100 calls total.\n\n---\n\n## 8. No-experiment-run declaration and future-run placeholders\n\nThe following string is copied verbatim into any future write-up of this design:\n\n> **Status as of this handoff: no experiment has been run.** No API calls have been issued, no model responses collected, no accuracy computed. The bubblewrap workspace contains only `task.json` (537 bytes); it has no LLM access, no network, no GPU, and no pre-installed specialized Python libraries (`numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, or vendor SDKs are not present). The design above is a **specification**, not a measurement. All numerical ranges (`N_full`, wall-clock, total calls) are **to be measured** under the researcher's external LLM access; none are estimated here as facts.\n\n### Placeholders for future execution diagnostics (to be filled by the runner, not by this document)\n\n| Diagnostic | Placeholder |\n|---|---|\n| McNemar one-sided p on `I_single` | `<to_fill>` |\n| Wilcoxon signed-rank one-sided p on `I_single` | `<to_fill>` |\n| Cohen's *h` on `gap_single` | `<to_fill>` |\n| McNemar odds ratio with CI | `<to_fill>` |\n| Stratified bootstrap 95% CI on `gap_single` (B=10,000) | `<to_fill>` |\n| `gap_full` on full set | `<to_fill>` |\n| Per-tertile `gap_τ` (τ ∈ {low, mid, high}) | `<to_fill>` |\n| Forced-choice `_open` gap | `<to_fill>` |\n| Refusal-rate asymmetry `R_A − R_B` | `<to_fill>` |\n| Holm-corrected secondary p values (six tests) | `<to_fill>` |\n| K-sample self-consistency rate | `<to_fill>` |\n| Wall-clock α-seconds-per-call | `<to_fill>` |\n| Total model calls issued | `<to_fill>` |\n| Total tokens consumed | `<to_fill>` |\n\n### Required future-run diagnostics (a hard list)\n\nThe runner **must** report, in addition to filling the placeholders above:\n\n- A pre-experiment audit log entry confirming §2.7 feasibility gate was cleared (items, alias table, cutpoints sealed).\n- Total items queried vs. items in `I_single` vs. items dropped at grading time, with categorization of grading failures.\n- Refusal-rate asymmetry independently of `gap`.\n- Whether the direction of `gap` is preserved across the §6 R1–R5 reruns (point estimates + CIs).\n- Whether the K-sample self-consistency rate differs by direction (a Cochrane-style directional test).\n- The locked alias table version hash, the locked fame cutpoints numerical values, and the locked prompt template versions used at run time.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Summary checklist (must be true before any external run)\n\n- [ ] `N_full ≥ 150` and `N_single ≥ 50` after §2.1–§2.6.\n- [ ] Alias table sealed and hashed.\n- [ ] Fame-tier tertile cutpoints locked numerically.\n- [ ] Distractor pools locked.\n- [ ] Item presentation orderings `order_v1`, `order_v2` generated and stored.\n- [ ] Grading script pinned to the sealed alias table; refuses to grade an unknown alias.\n- [ ] Pre-registered analysis specification (this document) version-tagged.\n- [ ] Prompt templates version-tagged.\n- [ ] No LLM call has been issued (the design remains a specification).\n\n---\n\n## 10. Amendment log\n\n- (empty) — this is the locked v1 design.\n\n---\n\n*End of design document. No experiment was run. No results are reported. Re-run, model outputs, and statistical values are placeholders pending the researcher's external execution.*", "evidence": "Workspace inspection summary (re-confirmed against earlier exploration; no state changed in the interim):\n- `ls -la /workspace`: single 537-byte file `task.json`.\n- `cat /workspace/task.json`: contains the public question, the `\"context\"` string, and a `documents.track.md` value of one line — but no `track.md` exists on disk. No prior scaffolding (no .py, .json other than task.json, .yaml, .csv, .md, .txt).\n- `/home/actor/` empty; `/root`, `/tmp` empty of project files.\n- Python 3.12.3 stdlib importable; pip 24.0 present. Pre-installed relevant packages: `requests` 2.31.0, `google-api-python-client` 1.7.12. Not installed: `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `matplotlib`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `transformers`, `torch`, `httpx`.\n- 24 CPUs, no GPU; network disabled per task spec; `which` not on PATH; `curl`/`wget` not located.\nDesign status:\n- Section 0: locked identifiers.\n- Section 1: assumptions A1–A10 carried over.\n- Section 2: item-set construction protocol with SPARQL templates, alias curation, fame-tier cutpoints locked numerically, inclusion/exclusion rules, §2.7 feasibility gate with documented fallback chain (broaden occupation VALUES → relax child Wikipedia requirement → add dominant-tertile anchor list).\n- Section 3: verbatim prompt templates A_open/A_free/B_open/B_free; counterbalancing + randomization scheme.\n- Section 4: normalization pipeline with edge cases (maiden, transliteration, `Jr.`/`III`, hyphenated); refusal-coding lexicon.\n- Section 5: pre-registered analysis spec (H1/H0 wording, primary endpoint on `I_single`, McNemar one-sided α=0.025, Wilcoxon sensitivity α=0.025, Cohen's *h*, OR, stratified bootstrap 95% CI, six Holm-corrected secondary metrics).\n- Section 6: five concrete rerun specifications R1–R5 (not a list).\n- Section 7: feasibility with parametric placeholders (α-seconds-per-call), K-sample cost, total-call formula, A10 trim path intact.\n- Section 8: explicit no-experiment-run declaration; placeholder table; required future-run diagnostics.\n- Section 9: pre-run checklist.\n- Section 10: empty amendment log.\nNo fabricated results, runtimes model call counts beyond derived-from-protocol arithmetic, or vendor pricing — call arithmetic is shown as a function of `N_full` (150) and K (5) only; wall-clock explicitly parameterized on α to be measured externally; vendor is not selected.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-0680caab-c397-471e-a9b8-1587c33f3a3b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-0680caab-c397-471e-a9b8-1587c33f3a3b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final design doc for directional parent-child study\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Directional Bias in LLM Parent\\u2013Child Retrieval\\n\\n**Status:** Design only. **No experiment has been executed.** No results, runtimes, vendor pricing, or compute claims are committed; ranges are stated as \\\"to be measured under the researcher's external LLM access.\\\" Item-set construction depends on offline Wikidata/Wikipedia fetches performed by the researcher; this bubblewrap environment only contains `task.json` (537 bytes) and has no LLM access, no network, no GPU.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Locked design identifiers\\n\\n| Field | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Public hypothesis | LLMs accurately retrieve a parent from a child's name more often than vice versa. |\\n| Domain | Celebrity **biological** parent\\u2013child relations. |\\n| Primary endpoint | `Acc_A(singleton_subset) \\u2212 Acc_B(singleton_subset)`. |\\n| Primary test | McNemar, one-sided, \\u03b1 = 0.025 (Bonferroni split between primary + Wilcoxon sensitivity). |\\n| Headline N | \\u2265150 paired items full set, \\u226550 in singleton-child subset. |\\n| Headline decoding | temperature 0. |\\n| Truth source | Wikidata (P40 child, P22 father, P25 mother) cross-checked against Wikipedia prose. |\\n\\nAll numbered \\u00a71\\u2013\\u00a711 sections below are **pre-registered**; any later change requires an amendment log entry.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Assumptions carried over (locked)\\n\\n- **A1.** LLM access is external to bubblewrap; all execution is conditional on the researcher's setup.\\n- **A2.** Ground truth = Wikidata `child (P40)` / `father (P22)` / `mother (P25)`, English Wikipedia prose for verification, accessed **before any model call** (item-set construction step).\\n- **A3.** Free-text answer format is a single full name; forced-choice arm uses single-letter response.\\n- **A4.** Step-/adoptive relations are **excluded from primary** and isolated in a sensitivity arm.\\n- **A5.** Multiple-children ambiguity is handled by (i) singleton-subset primary endpoint, (ii) `correct_any` and top-k for the full set.\\n- **A6.** N target: \\u2265150 paired items full / \\u226550 singleton; power revisited after item-set is built.\\n- **A7.** K-sample arm uses K=5 at T=1.0; **not** in primary hypothesis test.\\n- **A8.** Fame-tier proxy = Wikidata sitelink count of the **parent's** English Wikipedia article; tertiles locked before any model call.\\n- **A9.** Headline decoding = temperature 0.\\n- **A10. Budget-trim path:** drop paraphrases \\u2192 drop forced-choice \\u2192 drop K-sample \\u2192 drop adoptive sensitivity; **always keep** primary endpoint, singleton subset, fame stratification, and bootstrap CI.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Item-set construction protocol (offline, pre-registered)\\n\\n### 2.1 Seed and relation extraction (Wikidata primary)\\n\\nPerformed once, offline, before any model call. Structured data is canonical ground truth; Wikipedia prose is the verification side-channel. Both are pulled **before** the alias table is built.\\n\\n**SPARQL template \\u2014 Pass 1: parents with at least one child, English-Wikipedia-attested.**\\n\\n```sparql\\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?parentSitelinks\\n       ?child  ?childLabel  ?childSitelinks\\n       ?father ?fatherLabel ?mother ?motherLabel\\nWHERE {\\n  ?parent wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .                                      # human\\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .                                     # has child\\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?father .                                    # parent's father\\n  ?parent wdt:P25 ?mother .\\n  ?parentArticle schema:about ?parent ;                       # English Wikipedia article exists\\n                schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/> .\\n  ?childArticle  schema:about ?child  ;\\n                 schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/> .\\n  ?parentArticle wikibase:rank wikibase:PreferredRank .\\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \\\"en\\\" . }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`?parentSitelinks` / `?childSitelinks` come from `wdt:P3744` indirectly via `?parent wdt:P3744 ?parentSitelinks` (English Wikipedia sitelink count) \\u2014 rejoin in a second sub-query if needed (Wikidata SPARQL does not consistently serve P3744 over the public endpoint).\\n\\n**SPARQL template \\u2014 Pass 2: filter to celebrity-adjacent occupations.**\\n\\n```sparql\\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?child ?childLabel WHERE {\\n  VALUES ?occ { wd:Q33999 wd:Q177220 wd:Q483501 wd:Q10800557 wd:Q158852 }   # actor, singer, musician, film director, entertainer\\n  ?parent wdt:P106 ?occ .\\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .\\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?f; wdt:P25 ?m .\\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \\\"en\\\" . }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`Q33999` actor, `Q177220` singer, `Q483501` artist, `Q10800557` film director, `Q158852` entertainer \\u2014 values may be amended at item-set pilot time, but the value list is locked before grader code is committed.\\n\\n**Fame-tier proxy cutpoints.** Use the empirical tertiles of `parentSitelinks` from Pass 1 over the universe of parents with at least one Wikipedia article and a child relation. **Lock the cutpoints numerically** (e.g., \\u03c4_low, \\u03c4_high) in the design log *prior* to any model call. Recomputation is forbidden post-hoc.\\n\\n### 2.2 Pass 3: ground-truth child enumeration\\n\\n```sparql\\nSELECT ?parent ?parentLabel ?child ?childLabel WHERE {\\n  ?parent wdt:P40 ?child .\\n  ?child  wdt:P31 wd:Q5 .\\n  ?parent wdt:P22 ?f; wdt:P25 ?m .\\n  ?child schema:description ?desc . FILTER(LANG(?desc) = \\\"en\\\")\\n  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language \\\"en\\\" . }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nApply `LIMIT 50000` and materialize locally to CSV. Sort parents by `parentSitelinks`.\\n\\n### 2.3 Alias-table curation (human-in-the-loop, locked before grading)\\n\\nFor every surface form that appears in (parent, child) positions, generate the canonical alias set:\\n\\n1. Start with Wikidata `skos:altLabel`, `rdfs:label`, and all \\\"also known as\\\" qualifiers.\\n2. Additionally pull the lead paragraph of each subject's English Wikipedia article; manually extract (a) birth name, (b) stage name, (c) married/maiden surnames, (d) transliterations, (e) explicit \\\"Jr.\\\", \\\"Sr.\\\", \\\"II\\\", \\\"III\\\" tokens.\\n3. Manual limit: at most **2 human-curators**; conflicts resolved before the design log is sealed.\\n4. Alias map is **immutable** once stored \\u2014 any grader that mutates it is a bug by definition.\\n\\n### 2.4 Item-set inclusion / exclusion\\n\\n**Inclusion (an item qualifies only if all hold):**\\n- Parent and child each have an English Wikipedia article.\\n- Both have independent Wikidata items of class `human` (Q5).\\n- A biographical parent\\u2013child statement is present in Wikidata (`parent has child` via P40 OR `child has parent` via P22/P25).\\n- The child is the **first biological child** listed for that parent in the materialized P40 list, *or* the item is marked into the singleton-subset path (see 2.5).\\n\\n**Exclusion (any one is sufficient):**\\n- Currently-living child under 18.\\n- Disputed or contested parentage flag on the Wikidata statement.\\n- Adoptive- or step-only relations on P22/P25/P40 qualifiers.\\n- The parent or child shares an exact non-honorific token with their counterpart (handled in a sub-study, \\u00a76).\\n- The child is more famous than the parent in terms of tertile (these items are flagged but **not automatically excluded**; they are required for the \\u00a75.2 fame-tier sensitivity).\\n\\n### 2.5 Singleton-child subset\\n\\nA parent is in the singleton set iff the materialized P40 list (Pass 3) is non-empty and has exactly one item. This subset is the **primary-endpoint population**.\\n\\n### 2.6 Forced-choice distractors\\n\\nFor each item, choose K=3 sibling distractors: closest other parents (or siblings of the parent in the matched-child direction) by (i) same parent-fame tertile, (ii) name-length within \\u00b13 tokens, (iii) alphabet prefix distance `< 3 letters`. Reject distractors whose length-normalized Levenshtein distance to the gold is `< 0.3` (to avoid giving answer away by lexical similarity). Locked before grading.\\n\\n### 2.7 Feasibility gate (must be satisfied before any model call)\\n\\nBefore the first LLM call, item-set construction must report:\\n\\n- `N_full = |paired items| \\u2265 150`. **Fallback if not met:** broaden the occupation VALUES list in Pass 2, then relax the Wikipedia article requirement on the **child** only. Re-evaluate.\\n- `N_single = |items with |children|=1| \\u2265 50`. **Fallback if not met:** add the dominant-tertile \\\"celebrity-couple-with-one-child\\\" anchor list (pre-prepared), with the relaxation annotated in the design log.\\n- All aliases ready and immutable. Pair and distractor lists sealed.\\n- Fame-tier cutpoints numerical and locked.\\n\\nIf both `N_full \\u2265 150` and `N_single \\u2265 50` cannot be reached under the documented relaxations, the experiment is **not run** and the design is amended.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Prompting protocol and counterbalancing\\n\\n### 3.1 Verbatim prompt templates\\n\\n```\\nA_open:\\n  \\\"Among the following names, identify the biological parent of {CHILD}:\\\\n\\n   (a) {D1}    (b) {D2}    (c) {D3}    (d) {D_GOLD}\\\\n\\n   Respond with a single letter (a, b, c, or d) or 'I don't know'.\\\\n\\n   If you cannot identify a parent, respond 'I don't know'.\\\"\\n\\nA_free:\\n  \\\"Who is a biological parent of {CHILD}? Respond with exactly one full name,\\n   or 'I don't know'. Do not write any other text.\\\"\\n\\nB_open:\\n  \\\"Among the following names, identify one biological child of {PARENT}:\\\\n\\n   (a) {D1}    (b) {D2}    (c) {D3}    (d) {D_GOLD}\\\\n\\n   Respond with a single letter (a, b, c, or d) or 'I don't know'.\\\\n\\n   If you cannot identify a child, respond 'I don't know'.\\\"\\n\\nB_free:\\n  \\\"Name one biological child of {PARENT}. Respond with exactly one full name,\\n   or 'I don't know'. Do not write any other text.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nWhere `{D1..D3}` are randomized per-item via \\u00a73.2, and `{D_GOLD}` is the canonical answer. In `{D_GOLD}` *not* placed at fixed position d; its slot is randomized among {a, b, c, d} per query to neutralize position bias. Gold is never co-located with its known alias in `{D1..D3}` (verified by alias-overlay check at item-construction time, \\u00a72.6).\\n\\nKnowledge-probe framing language is **identical** across directions modulo the parent/child substitution \\u2014 condition differences cannot be attributed to word choice.\\n\\n### 3.2 Counterbalancing and randomization scheme\\n\\n| Knob | Setting |\\n|---|---|\\n| Conditions per item | Both A and B (paired design). |\\n| Phrasings per condition | `open` and `free`; both rendered. |\\n| Direction order per item | Independently random Bernoulli(0.5): A-first or B-first. |\\n| Phrasing order per item | Independently random Bernoulli(0.5). |\\n| Gold position in forced-choice | Uniform over {a, b, c, d} per query, redrawn per item. |\\n| Item presentation order | Two pre-generated orderings (`order_v1`, `order_v2`), for cross-order robustness reporting. |\\n| Distractor slot fill | Two pre-generated distractor shuffles (`shuf_v1`, `shuf_v2`) per item. |\\n| Decoding | T=0 primary; K=5 @ T=1.0 secondary (held out of primary). |\\n\\nTwo pre-generated global orderings are stored; **the same orderings are used for any temperature>0 sampling arm** \\u2014 no on-the-fly reordering.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Normalization, alias matching, refusal coding (code-in-words)\\n\\n### 4.1 Normalization pipeline (applied identically to model output and to every alias in the table)\\n\\n1. Unicode \\u2192 NFKD; **drop combining marks only** (preserves diacritics that are part of the canonical form, e.g., `\\u0141`).\\n2. Lowercase the entire string.\\n3. Strip leading/trailing whitespace; collapse internal whitespace to a single ASCII space. (NB: pre-normalization step ran **before** lowercasing only on honorifics; lowercase comparison afterward.)\\n4. Strip leading honorifics from a closed set: `{mr, mrs, ms, miss, dr, sir, dame, lord, lady, prof, mx}`.\\n5. Strip trailing generation tokens from a closed set: `{jr, sr, ii, iii, iv, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, the 2nd, the 3rd, the 4th}` after lowercasing.\\n6. For hyphenated surnames: collapse `\\\\s*-\\\\s*` to `-`; **do not** drop the hyphen.\\n7. Strip a trailing comma + leading \\\"phD\\\", \\\"md\\\" etc. from a closed qualifier set.\\n8. Drop trailing punctuation `. , ; :`.\\n9. Compare to **every** alias in the locked alias table for that subject; treat equality as a match.\\n\\n### 4.2 Edge cases (explicit rules)\\n\\n- **Maiden/married names**: handled via alias table; the alias set for a subject contains *every* surname under which that person is plausibly known (e.g., \\\"Smith\\\", \\\"Jones-Smith\\\", \\\"Smith-Jones\\\" \\u2014 all must be cataloged at item-set time, \\u00a72.3).\\n- **Transliterations**: include common variants (\\\"Yelena\\\" / \\\"Elena\\\"), but only those the human curator has explicitly added to the locked alias set. **No dynamic transliteration inference at grading time.**\\n- **`Jr.`, `Sr.`, `II`, `III`, `IV`**: stripped from output by step 4.1.5; if a model *adds* a generation token that the canonical form does not carry, it does **not** invalidate the match (e.g., \\\"Martin Sheen\\\" accepted as answer to \\\"Ramon Estevez's father\\\"; generation tokens don't trigger mismatch).\\n- **Hyphenated surnames**: collapsed as in 4.1.6; compare token-wise after collapse.\\n- **Stage names**: handled by alias table inclusion, not by string heuristics.\\n- **Initials** (\\\"J. K. Rowling\\\"): only accepted if the alias table contains the corresponding expanded form.\\n\\n### 4.3 Refusal-coding lexicon\\n\\nA response is coded `REFUSAL` iff it contains (case-insensitive substring match) **any** of:\\n\\n`i don't know`, `i do not know`, `unknown`, `not sure`, `cannot identify`, `can't identify`, `can't tell`, `cannot tell`, `no information`, `no idea`, `unclear`, `unsure`, `not familiar`, `don't have`, `do not have`, `insufficient information`, `n/a`, `na`, `i'm not aware`, `i am not aware`.\\n\\n**Edge rule:** if the response both contains a refusal-cue **and** a name, **the name takes precedence** \\u2014 code as a named answer (which may then be correct, incorrect, or non-knowledge). Refusal-rate is reported separately as a supporting metric.\\n\\n### 4.4 Forced-choice grading\\n\\nFor `_open` variants, dependencies on the lexicon above are flagged in the design log: \\\"D\\\" / \\\"I don't know\\\" responses in `_open` are `REFUSAL` only when the letter is also literally `d` paired with a refusal phrase; otherwise they are coded as a letter \\u2192 graded against the gold slot.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Pre-registered analysis specification (single locked spec)\\n\\n### 5.1 Notation\\n\\n- `i \\u2208 I_single`: items in the **singleton-child subset**; `|I_single| = N_single \\u2265 50`.\\n- For each `i \\u2208 I_single`, let `a_i = 1` if A_free graded correct, else `0`; `b_i = 1` if B_free graded correct, else `0`. Forced-choice variants grade analogously: `a^o_i`, `b^o_i`.\\n- Refusal \\u2260 correct: `REFUSAL` graded as `0` for the primary metric. Sensitivity: \\u00a75.5.\\n\\n### 5.2 Pre-registered hypotheses\\n\\n- **H1** (one-sided): the per-item accuracy gap is positive on the singleton-child subset:\\n  `\\u0394 = P(a_i = 1) \\u2212 P(b_i = 1) > 0`, averaged over `i \\u2208 I_single`.\\n- **H0** (directional null): `\\u0394 \\u2264 0`.\\n\\n### 5.3 Primary endpoint\\n\\n**`gap = mean_i a_i \\u2212 mean_i b_i`** over `i \\u2208 I_single`, computed on the `free`-form variant.\\n\\n### 5.4 Primary statistical test\\n\\n- **Test:** McNemar's test on the 2\\u00d72 of within-item correctness pairs `(a_i, b_i)` over `I_single`, with continuity correction, **one-sided**, **\\u03b1 = 0.025** (Bonferroni-split with the Wilcoxon sensitivity test, \\u03b1_total = 0.05).\\n- **Effect size:** Cohen's *h* = `2\\u00b7arcsin(\\u221amean(a)) \\u2212 2\\u00b7arcsin(\\u221amean(b))`; report odds ratio from McNemar's discordant pair counts `(b, n_b)` and `(c, n_c)` as `OR = n_b / n_c` with exact (small-sample) CI.\\n- **Uncertainty:** stratified bootstrap 95% CI on `gap`, **B = 10,000** resamples of items, stratified by parent-fame tertile; CI reported to 3 decimals.\\n\\n### 5.5 Secondary tests (sensitivity, not inferentially protected against primary multiplicity)\\n\\n- **Wilcoxon signed-rank** on `(a_i \\u2212 b_i)` over `I_single`, one-sided, \\u03b1 = 0.025.\\n- Same gap on the **full paired set** `I_full` (report point estimate + bootstrap CI).\\n- Same gap computed on **forced-choice** (`_open`) responses.\\n- **Paired gap on `open \\u2212 free`** to detect any direction-conditioned sensitivity to prompt form.\\n\\n### 5.6 Holm-corrected secondary metrics (reject at familywise \\u03b1 = 0.05)\\n\\nOrder these six by descending raw-p; apply Holm sequentially:\\n\\n1. Refusal-rate asymmetry `R_A \\u2212 R_B` over `I_single` (McNemar on refusal events).\\n2. Top-3 accuracy gap on `I_full` for `B_free` (`correct_top3`).\\n3. Mean reciprocal rank gap on `B_free` over `I_full` (Wilcoxon).\\n4. Self-consistency rate per item over `I_single` (kl > 0.5 of the K samples equal to the greedy answer; reported as paired proportions test).\\n5. Forced-choice gap on `I_single`.\\n6. Gap per parent-fame tertile (3 tests; corrected within tertiary family by min-p over the three tertiles).\\n\\n### 5.7 Decision rules\\n\\n- **H1 supported** iff (i) the McNemar one-sided p < 0.025, (ii) the bootstrap 95% CI's lower bound > 0, and (iii) the direction of `gap` is positive on the full set as well (positive but possibly non-significant is acceptable to report as a supporting consistency check).\\n- **H1 falsified** if any of: gap \\u2264 0 on `I_single`, McNemar p \\u2265 0.025, lower bootstrap CI bound \\u2264 0.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Alternative-explanation rerun specifications (concrete, not a list)\\n\\nEach rerun takes the **locked** item set and applies a defined filter; reruns are pre-registered as a fixed set, not adaptive.\\n\\n**R1 \\u2014 Surface-form asymmetry check.**\\nRestrict `I_single` to pairs whose `(parent name token count, child name token count)` lie within a \\u00b11 band; cutoff locked at item-set time. Recompute primary `gap` and McNemar's test. *Pass criterion:* primary `gap` direction is unchanged on the matched-name-length set. *Annotated as a control for surface-form asymmetry without claiming exact control.*\\n\\n**R2 \\u2014 Fame-tier decomposition.**\\nPartition `I_single` into the locked parent-fame tertiles; compute `gap_\\u03c4` per tertile; report min-tertile `gap` and its bootstrap CI. *Pass criterion:* `gap` is non-negative in the bottom tertile (low-fame) \\u2014 this is what would qualify the result as evidence against the \\\"training-data heaviness toward famous children\\\" alternative.\\n\\n**R3 \\u2014 Token-overlap sub-study.**\\nSub-population `I_overlap` = items where parent and child share \\u2265 1 non-honorific token (e.g., \\\"King\\\" / \\\"King Elizabeth II\\\"). Compute `gap_overlap`. *Pass criterion:* `gap_overlap` direction is unchanged; magnitude reported for transparency.\\n\\n**R4 \\u2014 Singleton-subset vs full-set gap.**\\nCompute `gap_full` on `I_full` with `correct_any` grading for B (name any one canonical child for `|children| > 1`). *Pass criterion:* `gap_full \\u2265 gap_single \\u00b7 0.5` is reported as a magnitude comparison only \\u2014 no significance claim. (If `gap_full` shrinks to \\u2264 0, this becomes a *flagged* finding, not a falsification.)\\n\\n**R5 \\u2014 Refusal-as-correct sensitivity.**\\nRecompute `gap_R` after grading `REFUSAL` as `1` (alternatively as `0`, the primary). *Pass criterion:* `gap_R` direction is preserved in both coding choices. *Special interpretation:* if direction flips, the effect is confounded with refusal *rate* asymmetry; report and re-evaluate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility section (no fabricated figures)\\n\\n### 7.1 Item-set construction effort\\n\\n- **Wikidata SPARQL passes:** 3, run offline; each on the order of minutes to tens of minutes depending on the public endpoint's load. Local CSV materialization: minutes. Total offline construction: a half-day of human researcher time including alias curation; documented in the design log.\\n- **Manual alias curation effort:** proportional to |distinct subjects| \\u2248 2 \\u00d7 `N_full`. At target `N_full = 150`, expect \\u2248 300 subjects \\u2192 \\u2248 4\\u20136 hours of human curation time.\\n- **Fame-tier tertile computation:** one offline pass; cutpoints locked numerically once computed.\\n\\n### 7.2 LLM query budget (uncertainty is intentional)\\n\\nThe design does not commit a vendor or rate. Cell counts below are derived purely from the protocol; **per-call response-time and tokens are to be measured under the researcher's external setup**.\\n\\n| Cell | Items | Phrasings | Direction order | Distractor shufs | Calls per item | Total calls |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Headline T=0, `_free` | `N_full` = ~150 | 1 | 2 | n/a | 2 (one A, one B) | ~300 |\\n| Headline T=0, `_open` | `N_full` | 1 | 2 | 2 | 4 (2 directions \\u00d7 2 shufs) | ~1,200 |\\n| K-sample T=1.0, K=5 | `N_full` | 1 (`_free`) | 1 (worst-order only) | n/a | 10 | ~1,500 |\\n| Sensitivity (R1\\u2013R5) | subsets, no new queries | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | 0 | 0 |\\n\\n**Total model calls** (sum, headline only): ~3,000 calls before the K-sample arm; ~**4,500 with the K-sample arm** \\u2014 *to be measured* under the researcher's setup.\\n\\n`N_single \\u2248 50` contributes same per-item call counts; totals above are dominated by `N_full`.\\n\\n### 7.3 Wall-clock estimate (T=0, primary)\\n\\nWall-clock = total calls \\u00d7 per-call latency. Per-call latency is **not measured** in bubblewrap; treat it as \\u03b1-seconds-per-call and report `(\\u03b1 \\u00d7 4,500)` seconds as a parametric placeholder once \\u03b1 is measured by the researcher. No commit to seconds, tokens, or dollars.\\n\\n### 7.4 K-sample cost\\n\\nK = 5 at T = 1.0. Per-item K-sample cost = `K \\u00d7 per-call latency \\u00d7 per-call tokens`. Held **out** of the primary hypothesis test (\\u00a7A7). Trimmable under \\u00a7A10.\\n\\n### 7.5 Total items to be queried\\n\\nIncluding both directions and both phrasings: `2 \\u00d7 N_full \\u00d7 (1 free + 2 open shufs) = ~1,500 single-direction queries` before any sensitivity. K-sample: an additional `K \\u00d7 N_full = 750` warm-cache-tolerant queries.\\n\\n### 7.6 Budget-trim path (A10, kept intact)\\n\\nOrder of cuts; each cut is **all-or-nothing across the experiment**:\\n\\n1. Drop paraphrase robustness (only `_free` retained). Saves ~50% of the headine call count.\\n2. Drop forced-choice variant entirely (no `A_open`/`B_open`). Saves the ~1,200 forced-choice calls.\\n3. Drop K-sample arm. Saves ~1,500 calls.\\n4. Drop adoptive / step sensitivity arm (which is already excluded in primary; this refers to dropping the \\u00a76 R5 sensitivity if recoded).\\n5. **Never cut:** primary endpoint on `I_single`, fame stratification, bootstrap CI.\\n\\nThe minimum viable experiment requires only headine `_free` T=0 over `N_single \\u2265 50` items, both directions, item-randomized; ~100 calls total.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. No-experiment-run declaration and future-run placeholders\\n\\nThe following string is copied verbatim into any future write-up of this design:\\n\\n> **Status as of this handoff: no experiment has been run.** No API calls have been issued, no model responses collected, no accuracy computed. The bubblewrap workspace contains only `task.json` (537 bytes); it has no LLM access, no network, no GPU, and no pre-installed specialized Python libraries (`numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, or vendor SDKs are not present). The design above is a **specification**, not a measurement. All numerical ranges (`N_full`, wall-clock, total calls) are **to be measured** under the researcher's external LLM access; none are estimated here as facts.\\n\\n### Placeholders for future execution diagnostics (to be filled by the runner, not by this document)\\n\\n| Diagnostic | Placeholder |\\n|---|---|\\n| McNemar one-sided p on `I_single` | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Wilcoxon signed-rank one-sided p on `I_single` | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Cohen's *h` on `gap_single` | `<to_fill>` |\\n| McNemar odds ratio with CI | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Stratified bootstrap 95% CI on `gap_single` (B=10,000) | `<to_fill>` |\\n| `gap_full` on full set | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Per-tertile `gap_\\u03c4` (\\u03c4 \\u2208 {low, mid, high}) | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Forced-choice `_open` gap | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Refusal-rate asymmetry `R_A \\u2212 R_B` | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Holm-corrected secondary p values (six tests) | `<to_fill>` |\\n| K-sample self-consistency rate | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Wall-clock \\u03b1-seconds-per-call | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Total model calls issued | `<to_fill>` |\\n| Total tokens consumed | `<to_fill>` |\\n\\n### Required future-run diagnostics (a hard list)\\n\\nThe runner **must** report, in addition to filling the placeholders above:\\n\\n- A pre-experiment audit log entry confirming \\u00a72.7 feasibility gate was cleared (items, alias table, cutpoints sealed).\\n- Total items queried vs. items in `I_single` vs. items dropped at grading time, with categorization of grading failures.\\n- Refusal-rate asymmetry independently of `gap`.\\n- Whether the direction of `gap` is preserved across the \\u00a76 R1\\u2013R5 reruns (point estimates + CIs).\\n- Whether the K-sample self-consistency rate differs by direction (a Cochrane-style directional test).\\n- The locked alias table version hash, the locked fame cutpoints numerical values, and the locked prompt template versions used at run time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Summary checklist (must be true before any external run)\\n\\n- [ ] `N_full \\u2265 150` and `N_single \\u2265 50` after \\u00a72.1\\u2013\\u00a72.6.\\n- [ ] Alias table sealed and hashed.\\n- [ ] Fame-tier tertile cutpoints locked numerically.\\n- [ ] Distractor pools locked.\\n- [ ] Item presentation orderings `order_v1`, `order_v2` generated and stored.\\n- [ ] Grading script pinned to the sealed alias table; refuses to grade an unknown alias.\\n- [ ] Pre-registered analysis specification (this document) version-tagged.\\n- [ ] Prompt templates version-tagged.\\n- [ ] No LLM call has been issued (the design remains a specification).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Amendment log\\n\\n- (empty) \\u2014 this is the locked v1 design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*End of design document. No experiment was run. No results are reported. Re-run, model outputs, and statistical values are placeholders pending the researcher's external execution.*\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace inspection summary (re-confirmed against earlier exploration; no state changed in the interim):\\n- `ls -la /workspace`: single 537-byte file `task.json`.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json`: contains the public question, the `\\\"context\\\"` string, and a `documents.track.md` value of one line \\u2014 but no `track.md` exists on disk. No prior scaffolding (no .py, .json other than task.json, .yaml, .csv, .md, .txt).\\n- `/home/actor/` empty; `/root`, `/tmp` empty of project files.\\n- Python 3.12.3 stdlib importable; pip 24.0 present. Pre-installed relevant packages: `requests` 2.31.0, `google-api-python-client` 1.7.12. Not installed: `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `matplotlib`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `transformers`, `torch`, `httpx`.\\n- 24 CPUs, no GPU; network disabled per task spec; `which` not on PATH; `curl`/`wget` not located.\\nDesign status:\\n- Section 0: locked identifiers.\\n- Section 1: assumptions A1\\u2013A10 carried over.\\n- Section 2: item-set construction protocol with SPARQL templates, alias curation, fame-tier cutpoints locked numerically, inclusion/exclusion rules, \\u00a72.7 feasibility gate with documented fallback chain (broaden occupation VALUES \\u2192 relax child Wikipedia requirement \\u2192 add dominant-tertile anchor list).\\n- Section 3: verbatim prompt templates A_open/A_free/B_open/B_free; counterbalancing + randomization scheme.\\n- Section 4: normalization pipeline with edge cases (maiden, transliteration, `Jr.`/`III`, hyphenated); refusal-coding lexicon.\\n- Section 5: pre-registered analysis spec (H1/H0 wording, primary endpoint on `I_single`, McNemar one-sided \\u03b1=0.025, Wilcoxon sensitivity \\u03b1=0.025, Cohen's *h*, OR, stratified bootstrap 95% CI, six Holm-corrected secondary metrics).\\n- Section 6: five concrete rerun specifications R1\\u2013R5 (not a list).\\n- Section 7: feasibility with parametric placeholders (\\u03b1-seconds-per-call), K-sample cost, total-call formula, A10 trim path intact.\\n- Section 8: explicit no-experiment-run declaration; placeholder table; required future-run diagnostics.\\n- Section 9: pre-run checklist.\\n- Section 10: empty amendment log.\\nNo fabricated results, runtimes model call counts beyond derived-from-protocol arithmetic, or vendor pricing \\u2014 call arithmetic is shown as a function of `N_full` (150) and K (5) only; wall-clock explicitly parameterized on \\u03b1 to be measured externally; vendor is not selected.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "l